11. Deep repo review: some of the experimental claims need downgrading

This is important.

There is genuinely good mechanism work in this repo, but the documentation currently overstates several experimental results.
The Hermes “live agent preference” experiment isn't actually preference evidence

This is the biggest issue I found.

continuous_hermes_daemon.py calculates:

best = max similarity
worst = min similarity

before calling Hermes.

Then the Hermes prompt literally tells it the desired IDs:

Return JSON {"best": PRECOMPUTED, "worst": PRECOMPUTED}

And regardless of what Hermes responds, the code executes:

record_best_worst(
    precomputed_best,
    precomputed_worst
)

So Hermes's output is not used to choose anything.

Therefore the live logs demonstrate:

Hermes executable works
model fallback works
timing works
logging works
store works

but not:

Hermes truthfully chooses blind providers.

I'd downgrade H7 from PARTIAL to:

    PLUMBING ONLY — preference experiment not yet executed.

That's an easy fix.
12. The scarce-reveal result is interesting, but it is still synthetic

The reported result:

2-reveal:
best == purchase 79.6%

full reveal:
best == purchase 100%

is interesting because it expresses the quality-vs-price distinction we want.

But the underlying logs are synthetic personas with generated similarity/quality/price values.

And feedback_simulation.py explicitly hardcodes assumptions such as:

base low effort probability = 30%
consequential low effort = 2%

so that simulation cannot itself establish that consequential choice really reduces low-effort behaviour from 30% to 2%. That's an assumption fed into the model.

Therefore:

    5→2→1 is an excellent hypothesis and mechanism. It is not yet empirically proven by real agents.

The next experiment should be real.
13. There's also a problem with the “paid ranking is bad” experiment

The design invariant is excellent:

    sponsor money must not enter organic ranking.

And the unit test correctly verifies that adding a $1,000 campaign does not change organic_score.

But the simulation doesn't actually demonstrate that paid ranking hurts buyer utility.

The committed results show:

paid_rank_bad:
mean buyer utility ≈ 0.85768

separated_ids:
mean buyer utility ≈ 0.85640

So in this particular synthetic world, the deliberately “bad” paid-rank policy actually has slightly higher buyer utility.

Why?

Because the sponsored cold-start provider in the simulation is deliberately an actual hidden winner:

newseed:
coding 0.92
research 0.89
cheap $0.0012

So aggressively promoting the sponsor happens to work.

That doesn't invalidate the architecture.

It means the negative control needs:

great sponsor
average sponsor
bad sponsor
adversarial sponsor

× various budgets

Then measure whether sponsor-independent ranking remains robust.
14. One actual implementation bug/oddity in retrieval

The simpler EvidenceRetriever describes a D-optimal-ish allocation:

incumbent
closest
cheapest
uncertain
challenger

but its uncertain selector currently uses:

max(... provider_n ...)

which picks the provider with the most observations, not the least-known provider.

The richer SeparatedSlatePolicy has proper posterior uncertainty and is much closer to the intended mechanism.

So I'd treat retrieval.py as legacy/simple mode and converge production on slate.py.
15. The OPE architecture is surprisingly serious

This part I really like.

Arena already includes:

IPS
SNIPS
Doubly Robust
SWITCH-DR
slate propensity evaluation

for answering:

    “Would a new routing policy have performed better on historical traffic without spending real wallets testing it?”

That is exactly the kind of infrastructure a general x402 router eventually needs.

But production isn't there yet: slate.py itself acknowledges the current inclusion probability is only a conservative proxy and says production must log the exact policy probability.

So the architecture is ahead of the evidence.

That's fine for where it is.
16. The x402 evidence layer is also still partly mock

ArenaEvidenceV1 is the right shape:

provider
payer
request hash
response hash
amount
tx hash
timestamp
provider signature

That's exactly what Arena needs because settlement alone doesn't prove what response was returned.

But the Python implementation currently generates providerSignature using a SHA-256 placeholder rather than a genuine signature.

The README is honest that the chain witness/escrow path is still at Sepolia/witness stage rather than live production.

Again: strong architecture, not finished trust infrastructure.
